# Licensing

Triptych uses separate licenses for project-created content and the software
and typesetting infrastructure listed below. These grants cover only
copyright and similar rights that contributors hold and can license.

## Project-Created Content: CC BY 4.0

Except where a file or the [third-party material notice](THIRD_PARTY.md) says
otherwise, project-created prose, annotations, research records, editorial
organization, document design, and translations or prayers identified as
project-created in the adjacent text or source record are licensed under the
[Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License](LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt).
This includes project-created content in the public Markdown files, in the
document sources beneath `src/gpt/`, and in the published PDFs beneath
`doc/gpt/`.

A reasonable attribution, unless a work supplies a more specific credit, is:

> Adapted from *Triptych: AI Driven Studies in Catholic Faith, Worship, and Law* by
> Triptych contributors, licensed under CC BY 4.0.

Preserve any more specific creator credit supplied with a work. When
reasonably practicable, link to the original work and the license and identify
any changes. Attribution does not imply that Triptych, its contributors, or
any ecclesiastical authority approves or endorses an adaptation.

## Listed Software and Typesetting Infrastructure: MIT

The following files are licensed under the [MIT License](LICENSES/MIT.txt):

- `Makefile`;
- `scripts/`;
- `requirements-public-alpha.txt`;
- `.gitignore` and `.gitattributes`;
- `src/gpt/common/preamble.tex`;
- `src/gpt/devotions/novenas/shared/novena-format.tex`;
- `src/gpt/devotions/novenas/shared/recitation-companion-format.tex`; and
- `src/gpt/theology/sacraments/summary-preamble.tex`.

Document-local TeX commands and files that mix typesetting with authored
content remain under the project-content terms unless they say otherwise.

## Excluded and Public-Domain Material

The licenses above do not relicense third-party material, even when it appears
inside a CC-licensed source file or PDF. Public-domain material remains public
domain; Triptych claims no exclusive right in its underlying wording. See
[Third-Party Material](THIRD_PARTY.md) for the operative exclusions and known
categories.

## Project Identity

No trademark or project-identity rights are granted. The Triptych name and
subtitle may be used as reasonably necessary for attribution or to describe a
work's origin, but not in a way that implies an adaptation is an official
Triptych publication or has been sponsored, approved, or endorsed by Triptych,
its contributors, or an ecclesiastical authority.
